erate training data and it's  a special class of models that I'll I'll  dive into and explain why they're why  they're unique and what kinds of best  practices you should take as you go into  this world so what exactly is different  about these kinds of models well to  start off set the precedent I think this  is gonna look very familiar to anybody  who's done machine learning this is a  pretty traditional process you start out  by collecting your data you process it  you train a model you validate that  model and you make a prediction and  maybe you put it into production and you  slap an API on top so you can generate  predictions  as needed maybe you go back and get more  data later and run through the process  again this time in a bit faster method  but the point here is that your data  collection is independent of the last  step of the the predictions by contrast  in what I call a sustainable machine  learning model there's two different  steps so the first thing is this last  six step deployment where you're  actually using the predictions you  generate so that might be you know  targets for people to contact or direct  advertising to it might be  recommendations for products or movies  or what have you it might also be  something like you know a recommendation  for somewhere to inspect or audit  something about data quality something  about for example in the city of Boston  we use a similar system to target our  restaurant inspections these are all  kinds of things where you're taking  these predictions you're immediately  putting them out into the world and  using them to feedback into that first  step that outcome and feature collection  and then the second thing that's  different here in the process is the  retraining and revalidation steps where  because you're doing this over and over  and over again you often end up handling  those differently than you would if you  were just doing it the first time  through for example by automating some  of these processes or simply you know  building in to your production ization  automatic updates as they go in a way  that you know will alert you if  something goes wrong but doesn't require  you to have hands on keyboards every  time that this gets updated so why is  this really that big a deal well a few  things one there's really no perfect way  to optimize for something like this you  know if you're just trying to build a  one-off model where your only goal is  the best accuracy possible you know the  best fit in that one time you run  through that's easy we've got plenty of  metrics for that you know anybody who's  spent more than 20 minutes looking at  scikit-learn who's probably dug into you  know the metrics module and really come  to understand a lot of things through  there but you know there's no one metric  for something like this because you're  getting into the world of what's called  the exploration exploitation trade-off  any of you have done work with  experimentation particularly getting  beyond just basic a/b testing into  things like multi armed bandits you're  probably familiar with this idea that  you know you you're optimizing  several things you want to do as well as  possible right now but you also actually  want to learn and so there's actually a  benefit to not you know exploiting your  model fully in one moment because if you  make errors you learn from them going  forward and ultimately there's no  perfect formal solution for these kinds  of models it's very you know I hate to  say have an artistic process in a way  because no two models are going to have  the exact same solution and you know  maybe maybe someday somebody will come  up with a better approach for this but  from from my own experience it's been a  lot of trial and error and figuring out  what is really the right way for any  particular application to deploy a model  like this and then the last thing that's  worth noting too is that you know once  you're getting into this world of  building these models that are  continuously updating and improving over  time you're building software which is  just a very different paradigm of  programming you know beyond what your  typical data science intro would teach  you so you're thinking about things like  version control and automation and  scheduling and continuous integration  and setting up alerts just you know in  case your models start performing much  better or much worse than they had  before which might be a sign something's  wrong so you have to consider a lot of  these different things as you go not  just you know the basics of making a  machine learning model so here I've got  a framework that I would recommend as a  starting point and some of these things  can can vary depending on your  particular use case but I found this to  be a useful way to approach these kinds  of models first step is research design  that's something I note here is a manual  process pen and paper doesn't literally  have to be pen and paper but the point  here is that before you ever start to  code it's critically important as it is  in every data science project but  particularly in these kinds of models to  think about what your research design is  in that you know what are you predicting  what what is it that you're modeling  what is the data generating process  looks like what is the application you  know how is this going to be sustainable  over time before you ever start writing  code it's important to think about these  and and particularly so in a model in  models like these where you know over  time if you're not paying attention  things can get worse and worse and worse  and often these are the kind of models  you use for critical  processes and infrastructure and so  forth in any data-driven organization  once you get through there you go  through a you know your pretty standard  process of development and then  production ization and then you deploy  and when you deploy again as I mentioned  earlier that's 