r file from  consumer databases from voter contact  histories and so forth and you use that  to generate a model that you can use to  create predictions for every other voter  in a state or in the country we used to  do that all the time we do  for things like not only who are you  going to vote for or are you likely to  to turnout but we'd also think about  things like you know debt modeling  demographics or issue positions or how  likely you are to become a volunteer you  know these are all different kinds of  political models that we make and the  ones that are based on surveys they  involved a lot of cost upfront and you  can't always do that in terms of just  having the resources to go with you  original data collection every time you  want to update a model so the benefit in  these sustainable designs is that you  get that information basically as just  an organic part of the process  the trade-off here is that the data is  not as good it's not you know this  perfect random sample you might get if  you go out and do a survey you know  instead you're getting data which you  know there is there's bias be baked into  because you're not going out and  collecting data at random that's the  whole point of you know doing the doing  targeting or you know tailored ad  content or what-have-you  you're not doing it at random this is  that exploration exploitation trade-off  I was talking about the key here is that  these biases if you're doing doing this  right can be overcome and there are two  basic things you need to think about one  is coverage namely that you know if you  focus too narrowly in in one particular  area you know for example if you are  doing fundraising targeting which one  that was one of the biggest models I  made at the DNC  that we directed all the the voter  direct mail fundraising so if you've  received mail from the DNC in the past  couple years asking for money I'm sorry  that was me basically you know this was  like the most important one of the most  important models we made and the thing  is is you know if you just drill down a  really narrow set of targets the problem  is you don't learn about all the people  you're not asking you don't learn what  they would do and I'll talk more about  that in a minute but the point here is  that you know your system model is not  going to be sustainable if you don't  have enough coverage to make your model  change course over time and try new  things  the other part is where I'll reliability  and again I'll give a very practical  example of this in a minute but the idea  is you know you're you're putting these  predictions out into the wild you're  rating data which isn't necessarily  coming from you know the same process  you might find if you were to just be  randomly casting about and collecting  original data you know there's some bias  baked into this and if you don't if you  don't understand the data generating  process that you're using the issue that  will come up is that the data that you  have will not be an accurate reflection  of the data that you don't have you know  the relationships in the real world if  they're not reflected in your data it  can lead you to wrong conclusions so  just to illustrate and I should say all  of these slides have already made it  available there on my github page I've  linked to them from Twitter so no need  to to write everything down and I may  breathe through a few of these and you  might want to go back and check in more  detail but so the first example when I  was talking about coverage is when  you're talking about narrowing down to  some kind of target universe like like  for fundraising or say if you're  directing different kinds of  advertisements or different kind of  recommendations you know if you find  early on hey this group of people  responds best to our fundraising appeals  or or this ad works way better than the  other ad and you just focus on that then  what's gonna happen is you're not going  to collect any kind of information that  would change what you think and what you  do so in this example you know these are  just very token non real data examples  but basically there's you know three  different clusters of potentially good  targets in here and what happens is the  first time you run the model it finds  one of them and the next time you run  the model it just drills down into that  one again and again and it's ignoring  these other potential areas and in the  real world one of the ways this this can  look like is when I started doing direct  marketing targeting at the DNC there was  actually a rule in place that nobody  nobody would send mail to somebody who  hasn't already donated if they're under  the age of 65 and this was based on  findings from earlier targeting which  found well hey look make sense from a  logical point of view that people who  are of retirement age are more likely to  read their mail and respond to it so  yeah I can I can see where somebody got  that idea that you know they sent out a  bunch of mail and found that people 65  and upward donate  much higher rates the problem is is they  stop mailing anybody under age 65 and  well the the there's a sustainability  problem when you are soliciting new  donors who are over the age of 65 they  you know it's a limited population and  that wouldn't be such a big deal if we  were a really small nonprofit sending  out you know five or ten thousand pieces  of mail we were sending out anywhere  between one in five million pieces of  mail every month so we can exhaust that  population really quickly but the other  thing of course is you know once they  get on your donor list  they don't necessarily stay on that  donor list for that long so the  long-term value of those new donors  you're bringing in actually isn't as  great as if you get somebody to become a  first-time donor when they're in their  30s or 40s or 50s but what we found is  you know I I came into this and said you  know what let's relax this let's walk it  back we didn't have any data from  anybody under the age of 65 but we said  well you know what let's basically  effectively treat those people has the  bottom of that age range see what  happens and turns out the first time we  sent mail our best-performing age group  was 50 to 64 because in part we'd  exhausted all the good targets from 65  and up because these were this was the  non donor targets once they donated for  the first time they were taken out of  the universe so it turns out over time  what had happened was actually a  different age range was better because  we'd exhausted all good targets and we  would have never figured that out if we  had only focused continually on that one  particular age range of 65 and up the  other problem you can run into is if you  put too much stake in data that's  generated from a biased process and here  we've kind of got the opposite situation  we actually have a pretty clear pattern  if you can see all the data but what's  been observed so far is actually a  non-random set and so the model here  says well okay we find these three  clusters so let's focus on those and it  keeps trying to focus on those and  ignores that broader pattern that's  actually there in a way it's the the  opposite of what we saw before it may  focus specifically on these different  areas but you know it's relying too  heavily on data that actually went in  that in practice is not  it's not performing the way you expect  so one thing that we worked on a lot at  the DNC another kind of sustainable  model was for volunteer recruitment this  is a model that we put together for  campaigns to target people who could  potentially become volunteers and you  know the dirty little secret of it is it  was a way to get campaigns to share  volunteer lists with each other because  campaigns are generally terrible at that  not only are they very protective of it  but generally the day after an election  all the staff goes and gets another 