hat the model was looking at this  bias data and saying well you know  Republicans are actually great  Democratic volunteers let's ask more of  them and of course that's not how it  works  so in this case the solution wasn't  anything fancy we just finally had to  realize you know what let's just drop  the Republicans from our training sample  because that's obviously an outlier but  that's the point I'm trying to make here  that sometimes if your model can get too  caught up on these biased data  generating processes you can actually  ignore the real patterns that are in  there and get too focused on things that  don't really work once you extend them  out and the important thing is is you  know you're going to make mistakes like  that some of them might not be so  obvious but even in that case you should  make sure your model is open to change  and if it doesn't work the way that you  intended it picks up on that and and  changes its predictions going forward so  how do you make your day too diverse  well here are some five options and they  roughly go from at the top here a sort  of research focused approach of  deliberately collecting you know more  widely applicable data all the way down  to building something into your model  which allows for you to collect diverse  data without actually going out of your  way to to do specific research data  collection so the first one is what I  call broad-based sampling and that's  basically just sort of bridging that gap  between pure research data collection  and you know collecting it organically  through your process so in our  restaurant inspection models in Boston  we we send inspectors out to go to  places that we think are likely to have  violations but we also have regular  annual inspections between one two three  times depending on the kind of  establishment so every you know every  place basically is going to get checked  at least once a year so what that does  is it introduces new data from places  where we don't have any reason to think  that they're a good target for  inspection but we're going to do it  anyways so what that does is it keeps  diverse data pool in there so that you  know if we find that hey there's a  particular kind of restaurant or or you  know a particular characteristic that  actually predicts food safety violations  really strongly that we haven't been  accounting for that will make its way  into our data  the second is supplementation bringing  in outside data that can help create  more variety so when we were doing  fundraising we would not only take our  you know the targets that we were  identifying through our model we would  also target people who came in from  outside donor lists from other  organizations you know things like say  Emily's list or the the Democratic  Congressional Campaign Committee we  might share list with them and so we add  people in the trouble here of course is  that's again you know there's a bias in  that selection we have to account for  that that can be taken into account in  your model to some degree you just have  to take things with a grain of salt and  and build that into your feature set but  what it does it allows you to bring in  information about people or about other  circumstances or whatever it is you're  modeling that you wouldn't otherwise be  getting if you just relied on your  models predictions the third is what I  call waiting to learn and that's trying  to build in this learning into your  process so remember I talked about the  exploration exploitation trade-off and  how hard it is to make metrics well here  you would deliberately prioritize  instances where you know you you need to  get more information so what that might  mean is you know when you're  establishing a target list or you're  picking which ad to show somebody or  anything like that you incorporate  directly the uncertainty in your current  prediction as a way to prioritize  collecting new information in cases  where you have a lot of uncertainty the  fourth of these is what I call rough  targeting and this is this is probably  the most hacky solution here but it but  it actually works so the first time we  rolled out those volunteer models that I  mentioned a few minutes ago you know we  had them to whatever level of precision  we needed but you know what we realized  was well if we put out what is typical  in politics which is you put out a score  of somewhere between zero and 100 and  you know maybe you  rounded or with one you know one decimal  place what's gonna happen immediately is  every campaign it gets access to this is  going to go and contact the people with  scores of 100 and those people's phones  are going to blow up and they're never  gonna answer them again because they got  called by every campaign you know within  a hundred miles on the same day and so  instead what we did is we realized well  you know there's two things here one we  just don't want to blow up the list and  make it not work anymore but also we  want to collect more data because this  is the first iteration and so what we  decided to do was to round the scores so  the first iteration of this we actually  made it a zero to ten score and rounded  them so that basically and these are  these are ranked scores so you know zero  whereas basically Republicans you're  likely Republicans you know and and you  had deciles of one to ten among the  likely Democrats and so what happened is  the top targets it was actually a pretty  broad group they were still you know top  ten percent but the campaigns weren't  able to distinguish between those and so  what it did is it forced them to bring  in people who you know they weren't the  bottom tier targets but they also  weren't the very top based on our model  and so what that meant was we got a  pretty wide swath of the electorate so  the next time we did this we were able  to make the model better and better and  better because we were getting pretty  pretty wide coverage in 