on't have  any good numbers but at least then we  know ok this is this is a person we  don't have any good numbers for you know  let's go out to our our consumer data  partners and source a new phone number  for him and that allowed us to really  turn that over quickly it also meant  that you know if our model was making  predictions like the number it said was  best  he's invest that was corrected very  quickly so finally to wrap up I just  want to offer a few basic  recommendations for making these sorts  of models one is in terms of features  the the key here is building in features  that create heterogeneity in your  predictions that you don't just as I  mentioned earlier drill down into one  particular area of your feature space so  some of this is incorporating things  like cyclical patterns over time but you  also want features that will change  whether that's you know truly dynamic  features you know from a external  sources that can change on a regular  basis or things like component models  where you you build in things like  models of say demographics you know we  built in models of like language  preference and household composition and  marital status and so forth and we  updated those models regularly and so  that would offer something that would  add some variety into our our other  models that you know when we would rerun  and rerun and rerun you know we'd have  variation in the predictions which meant  we would end up trying out  more of the more of the feature space  over time but I think most important is  incorporating individual histories so  that example of you know Ben's phone  number when I try to call Ben I want to  make sure that feeds back into the model  because that's something where you know  I don't just want to fix the model I  want to make sure the model fixes its  own predictions because that's the whole  point of doing these sustainable models  you know the the outcome of the model is  also your data generating process and so  by d