r file from  consumer databases from voter contact  histories and so forth and you use that  to generate a model that you can use to  create predictions for every other voter  in a state or in the country we used to  do that all the time we do  for things like not only who are you  going to vote for or are you likely to  to turnout but we'd also think about  things like you know debt modeling  demographics or issue positions or how  likely you are to become a volunteer you  know these are all different kinds of  political models that we make and the  ones that are based on surveys they  involved a lot of cost upfront and you  can't always do that in terms of just  having the resources to go with you  original data collection every time you  want to update a model so the benefit in  these sustainable designs is that you  get that information basically as just  an organic part of the process  the trade-off here is that the data is  not as good it's not you know this  perfect random sample you might get if  you go out and do a survey you know  instead you're getting data which you  know there is there's bias be baked into  because you're not going out and  collecting data at random that's the  whole point of you know doing the doing  targeting or you know tailored ad  content or what-have-you  you're not doing it at random this is  that exploration exploitation trade-off  I was talking about the key here is that  these biases if you're doing doing this  right can be overcome and there are two  basic things you need to think about one  is coverage namely that you know if you  focus too narrowly in in one particular  area you know for example if you are  doing fundraising targeting which one  that was one of the biggest models I  made at the DNC  that we directed all the the voter  direct mail fundraising so if you've  received mail from the DNC in the past  couple years asking for money I'm sorry  that was me basically you know this was  like the most important one of the most  important models we made and the thing  is is you know if you just drill down a  really narrow set of targets the problem  is you don't learn about all the people  you're not asking you don't learn what  they would do and I'll talk more about  that in a minute but the point here is  that you know your system model is not  going to be sustainable if you don't  have enough coverage to make your model  change course over time and try new  things  the other part is where I'll reliability  and again I'll give a very practical  example of this in a minute but the idea  is you know you're you're putting these  predictions out into the wild you're  rating data which isn't necessarily  coming from you know the same process  you might find if you were to just be  randomly casting about and collecting  original data you know there's some bias  baked into this and if you don't if you  don't understand the data generating  process that you're using the issue that  will come up is that the data that you  have will not be an accurate reflection  of the data that you don't have you know  the relationships in the real world if  they're not reflected in your data it  can lead you to wrong conclusions so  just to illustrate and I should say all  of these slides have already made it  available there on my github page I've  linked to them from Twitter so no need  to to write everything down and I may  breathe through a few of these and you  might want to go back and check in more  detail but so the first example when I  was talking about coverage is when  you're talking about narrowing down to  some kind of target universe like like  for fundraising or say if you're  directing different kinds of  advertisements or different kind of  recommendations you know if you find  early on hey this group of people  responds best to our fundraising appeals  or or this ad works way better than the  other ad and you just focus on that then  what's gonna happen is you're not going  to collect