ax this let's walk it  back we didn't have any data from  anybody under the age of 65 but we said  well you know what let's basically  effectively treat those people has the  bottom of that age range see what  happens and turns out the first time we  sent mail our best-performing age group  was 50 to 64 because in part we'd  exhausted all the good targets from 65  and up because these were this was the  non donor targets once they donated for  the first time they were taken out of  the universe so it turns out over time  what had happened was actually a  different age range was better because  we'd exhausted all good targets and we  would have never figured that out if we  had only focused continually on that one  particular age range of 65 and up the  other problem you can run into is if you  put too much stake in data that's  generated from a biased process and here  we've kind of got the opposite situation  we actually have a pretty clear pattern  if you can see all the data but what's  been observed so far is actually a  non-random set and so the model here  says well okay we find these three  clusters so let's focus on those and it  keeps trying to focus on those and  ignores that broader pattern that's  actually there in a way it's the the  opposite of what we saw before it may  focus specifically on these different  areas but you know it's relying too  heavily on data that actually went in  that in practice is not  it's not performing the way you expect  so one thing that we worked on a lot at  the DNC another kind of sustainable  model was for volunteer recruitment this  is a model that we put together for  campaigns to target people who could  potentially become volunteers and you  know the dirty little secret of it is it  was a way to get campaigns to share  volunteer lists with each other because  campaigns are generally terrible at that  not only are they very protective of it  but generally the day after an election  all the staff goes and gets another 