hat the model was looking at this  bias data and saying well you know  Republicans are actually great  Democratic volunteers let's ask more of  them and of course that's not how it  works  so in this case the solution wasn't  anything fancy we just finally had to  realize you know what let's just drop  the Republicans from our training sample  because that's obviously an outlier but  that's the point I'm trying to make here  that sometimes if your model can get too  caught up on these biased data  generating processes you can actually  ignore the real patterns that are in  there and get too focused on things that  don't really work once you extend them  out and the important thing is is you  know you're going to make mistakes like  that some of them might not be so  obvious but even in that case you should  make sure your model is open to change  and if it doesn't work the way that you  intended it picks up on that and and  changes its predictions going forward so  how do you make your day too diverse  well here are some five options and they  roughly go from at the top here a sort  of research focused approach of  deliberately collecting you know more  widely applicable data all the way down  to building something into your model  which allows for you to collect diverse  data without actually going out of your  way to to do specific research data  collection so the first one is what I  call broad-based sampling and that's  basically just sort of bridging that gap  between pure research data collection  and you know collecting it organically  through your process so in our  restaurant inspection models in Boston  we we send inspectors out to go to  places that we think are likely to have  violations but we also have regular  annual inspections between one two three  times depending on the kind of  establishment so every you know every  place basically is going to get checked  at least once a year so what that does  is it introduces new data from places  where we don't have any reason to think  that they're a good target for  inspection but we're going to do it  anyways so what that does is it keeps  diverse data pool in there so that you  know if we find that hey there's a  particular kind of restaurant or or you  know a particular characteristic that  actually predicts food safety violations  really strongly that we haven't been  accounting for that will make its way  into our data  the second is supplementation bringing  in outside data that can help create  more variety so when we were doing  fundraising we would not only take our  you know the targets that we were  identifying through our model we would  also target people who came in from  outside donor lists from other  organizations you know things like say  Emily's list or the the Democratic  Congressional Campaign Committee we  might share list with them and so we add  people in the trouble here of course is  that's again you know there's a bias in  that selection we have to account for  that that can be taken into account in  your model to some degree you just have  to take things with a grain of salt and  and build that into your feature set but  what it does it allows you to bring in  information about people or about other  circumstances or whatever it is you're  modeling that you wouldn't otherwise be  getting if you just relied on your  models predictions the third is what I  call waiting to learn and that's trying  to build in this learning into your  process so remember I talked about the  exploration exploitation trade-off and  how hard it is to make metrics well here  you would deliberately prioritize  instances where you know you you need to  get more information so what that might  mean is you know when you're  establishing a target list or you're  picking which ad to show somebody or  anything like that you incorporate  directly the uncertainty in your current  prediction as a way to prioritize  collecting new information in cases  where you have a lot of uncertainty the  fourth of these is what I call rough  targeting and this is this is probably  the most hacky solution here but it but  it actually works so the first time we  rolled out those volunteer models that I  mentioned a few minutes ago you know we  had them to whatever level of precision  we needed but you know what we realized  was well if we put out what is typical  in politics which is you put out a score  of somewhere between zero and 100 and  you know maybe you  rounded or with one you know one decimal  place what's gonna happen immediately is  every campaign it gets access to this is  going to go and contact the people with  scores of 100 and those people's phones  are going to blow up and they're never  gonna answer them again because they got  called by every campaign you know within  a hundred miles on the same day and so  instead what we did is we realized well  you know there's two things here one we  just don't want to blow up the list and  make it not work anymore but also we  want to collect more data because this  is the first iteration and so what we  decided to do was to round the scores so  the first iteration of this we actually  made it a zero to ten score and rounded  them so that basically and these are  these are ranked scores so you know zero  whereas basically Republicans you're  likely Republicans you know and and you  had deciles of one to ten among the  likely Democrats and so what happened is  the top targets it was actually a pretty  broad group they were still you know top  ten percent but the campaigns weren't  able to distinguish between those and so  what it did is it forced them to bring  in people who you know they weren't the  bottom tier targets but they also  weren't the very top based on our model  and so what that meant was we got a  pretty wide swath of the electorate so  the next time we did this we were able  to make the model better and better and  better because we were getting pretty  pretty wide coverage in the last of  these is what I call high churn modeling  which is you don't actually go out of  your way to collect diverse data but the  thing you do do is you make sure that as  soon as your model doesn't work the way  you plan or you learn something about a  particular case or a particular  individual that makes you want to change  your prediction you make sure your model  will respond to that so one of the  things we did was phone quality scores  this is what the DNC used to assign what  the best phone number is for any given  individual in the country they're about  two hundred million or so registered  active voter registrations in the  country a lot of those are outdated  there are only about 160 million actual  registered voters but in any case for  those 200 million active registrations  we had about 500 million phone numbers  so we need to figure out how to match  them up and what we did is we built  models for any given komban  of those two we basically would come up  with a prediction of how likely it is  that it's a working number and that if  somebody answers it's going to be the  person you want to get on the phone and  so the thing we did with this model is  we built it to rerun every night with  the results of all the day's calls made  by every campaign in the country that  was using the central voter contact  software so by the leading up to the  2014 election you know we were talking  about 10 million calls a week we're  filtering into this system and what we  would do is as soon as we would get a  case where you know if if I tried  calling Ben over there and you know  found out his numbers been disconnected  but I have another number for Ben unless  I know that number is also terrible  what's going to happen is within 24  hours I'm gonna swap out the new number  for the old number and so the next time  somebody tries to call Ben they'll be  calling on a different number and we'll  get information about that and maybe  that number sucks too and we don't have  any good numbers but at least then we  know ok this is this is a person we  don't have any good numbers for you know  let's go out to our our consumer data  partners and source a new phone number  for him and that allowed us to really  turn that over quickly it also meant  that you know if our model was making  predictions like the number it said was  best  he's invest that was corrected very  quickly so finally to wrap up I just  want to offer a few basic  recommendations for making these sorts  of models one is in terms of features  the the key here is building in features  that create heterogeneity in your  predictions that you don't just as I  mentioned earlier drill down into one  particular area of your feature space so  some of this is incorporating things  like cyclical patterns over time but you  also want features that will change  whether that's you know truly dynamic  features you know from a external  sources that can change on a regular  basis or things like component models  where you you build in things like  models of say demographics you know we  built in models of like language  preference and household composition and  marital status and so forth and we  updated those models regularly and so  that would offer something that would  add some variety into our our other  models that you know when we would rerun  and rerun and rerun you know we'd have  variation in the predictions which meant  we would end up trying out  more of the more of the feature space  over time but I think most important is  incorporating individual histories so  that example of you know Ben's phone  number when I try to call Ben I want to  make sure that feeds back into the model  because that's something where you know  I don't just want to fix the model I  want to make sure the model fixes its  own predictions because that's the whole  point of doing these sustainable models  you know the the outcome of the model is  also your data generating process and so  by doing that you know you don't want to  just make a better model you want to  make better predictions for the the  individual cases that you're predicting  for second part when you're actually  building the model you want one that's  going to incorporate the complexity of  different kinds of interactions between  your features  you know incorporating things like  nonlinear patterns so I have a huge fan  of of tree based models for these sorts  of things using ensembles retraining  frequently all of these things are based  on the idea of you know every time you  run the model you should get different  predictions that's what allows your  model to change and improve over time  and it also allows it to try more of the  possible feature space to keep your data  diverse and then lastly you know  evaluating your model as you go I like  to do what I call out of context  validation so this is different from  your typical out-of-sample validation  where you just hold out something that's  generated from the same data generating  process you know I think particularly  for these kinds of models it's actually  much more important to make sure that  they work outside of the data you're  using for training so for example rather  than if we're doing fundraising models  you know rather than just holding out a  random 10 or 20% of the total set of you  know of fundraising mailers that we had  sent over the past year or two what  instead we would do when we were when we  were testing out the model to make sure  it works is we'd hold out the most  recent couple months and build a model  based on the data up until then and so  that was the proxy for what we were  actually trying to do which is predict  how something's going to work in the  future  and that ended up telling us a very  different thing because it meant that  any patterns that changed we didn't have  any data to tell us what the patter the  temporal patterns would be beyond what  was in that that initial training set  and you know we would do this too  stage model obviously we'd retrain with  all the data after the fact but to just  make sure that everything we were we  were trying to do was working correctly  you know we do that pretty extensive  validation also going forward you know  you do need to stay on top of things  track how your model is doing over time  be willing to change it and I think one  of the things that's really important is  you know your model should should either  stay about the same or get slightly  better over time  you know if it's getting worse you  probably want to to mix something up try  something new you know it might just be  that you're you're using a algorithm  that's not covering enough of the  feature space say or you know you want  to add some more of writing your data  but also be worried if your model gets  too good because it might mean that  either there's something wrong in your  model or you know your balance of  exploration and exploitation is a little  off you know I think models that that  predict too well are probably the worst  case scenario I'd much rather have a  model again get worse then get  dramatically better because that often  means data leakage so particularly be on  the lookout for that and and make sure  that you know you've built that in your  system particularly if you're gonna  automate so the final bit I think this  applies to all of machine learning but  especially for these kinds of models  first understand your data understand  what that means in the real world what  the data generating processes how your  data and predictions are going to be  applied and make sure you you have an  understanding of that before you start  making a model because ultimately if  you're making a model of something you  don't understand your model is going to  be terrible so don't do that please  second one sanity checks you know I'm  not the sort of person who thinks  machine learning should be telling us a  lot of things we could never figu